@meta
id: 30-05
title: Drill — out to the definition and back
chapter: 30
type: drill
skills: ctags
par: 34
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: F12, read, Alt-Left
vimrc: set hidden
open: src/domain/order/order.provider.ts

@brief
The call to `issueRefund` is missing its `await`. Before you add one you
want to be sure the method really is async — so go and look, then come
back and fix the call.

Eight times: cursor onto the name, out to the definition, back, add
`await`, save. Nothing to work out. The point is that the round trip
stops feeling like a detour.

@keys
| `Ctrl-]` | to the definition of the word under the cursor |
| `Ctrl-T` | back |
| `^` | first non-blank of the line |

@hints
`/issueRefund` `Enter` puts the cursor on the name, which is what
`Ctrl-]` needs.
--
`Ctrl-T` lands you back on the same character you left, so `^i` is
enough to get in front of the call.

@solution
    /issueRefund<Enter>  Ctrl-]  Ctrl-T  ^ i await  <Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

By the eighth rep the out-and-back should cost you no thought at all.
That matters more than it sounds: the reason people guess at an API
instead of reading it is that reading it feels expensive. Two keys each
way is cheap enough that you stop guessing.

@start tags
!_TAG_FILE_FORMAT	2	//
!_TAG_FILE_SORTED	1	//
OrderProvider	src/domain/order/order.provider.ts	/^export class OrderProvider {$/;"	c
PaymentService	src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts	/^export class PaymentService {$/;"	c
issueRefund	src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts	/^  public async issueRefund(orderId: string) {$/;"	m	class:PaymentService

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    this.paymentService.issueRefund(order.id);

    return order;
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    await this.paymentService.issueRefund(order.id);

    return order;
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts
export class PaymentService {
  public async issueRefund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });

    return response.data.receiptId;
  }
}
